'''Kurosaki Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|黒崎県}}, ''Kurosaki-ken'') is a [[Prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located in the [[Nakayagikoshu region|Nakayagikoshu]] on the eastern coast of the island of [[Yagikoshu]], facing the [[Shendan Ocean]]. With a population of 1,034,624 as of 2026, it is one of the narrowest prefectures in Okaiken—a slender coastal strip squeezed between the Shendan Ocean and the mountains of the interior. The prefectural capital is the city of [[Kurosaki]], a booming deepwater port that has become one of the most economically dynamic cities on Yagikoshu's eastern seaboard.

Kurosaki Prefecture is defined by its relationship with the sea. Nearly every settlement in the prefecture is a coastal community, and the Shendan Ocean shapes the prefecture's economy, culture, and daily life. The prefecture is Okaiken's leading producer of electronics components outside the [[Aizawa]] corridor, and its fishing fleet is among the most productive on the Shendan coast. But the same ocean that sustains Kurosaki also threatens it: the prefecture is among the most seismically active in Okaiken, and its towns are protected by some of the nation's tallest seawalls.

== History ==

=== The port of Kurosaki ===
[[File:Kurosaki Deepwater Port.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The Port of Kurosaki, the fourth-busiest in Yagikoshu by cargo tonnage]]
The city of Kurosaki owes its existence to a geological accident. While the Shendan coast of Yagikoshu is characterised by dramatic tidal ranges and treacherous currents, the bay on which Kurosaki sits is sheltered by a natural breakwater of volcanic rock that dampens the tidal surge, creating one of the few naturally calm deepwater harbours on the eastern seaboard. This advantage was recognised early: the [[Kondo Records]] of 1347 describe the bay as {{nihongo|"the still water"|凪浦|Nagi-ura}}, and by the [[Honda Shogunate]] period, Kurosaki had become the primary naval and commercial port for the eastern Shendan trade.

The port's modern boom began in the 1960s, when the expansion of [[Toyozawa Metropolis]] overwhelmed its own harbour facilities. Kurosaki—only 80 km by rail from Toyozawa Central Station—offered deepwater berths capable of handling the new generation of container ships, without the extreme tidal fluctuations that made Toyozawa's own port unreliable for time-sensitive cargo. The {{nihongo|Kurosaki Deepwater Terminal|黒崎深水ターミナル|Kurosaki Shinsui Tāminaru}}, opened in 1972, transformed the city from a regional fishing port into one of the busiest commercial harbours in the Shendan Ocean basin.

=== Earthquakes and seawalls ===
[[File:Kurosaki Seawalls.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Kurosaki's 18.5-metre seawalls, among the tallest in Okaiken]]
Kurosaki's prosperity has always been shadowed by seismic danger. The prefecture sits on the {{nihongo|Kurosaki Fault|黒崎断層|Kurosaki Dansō}}, an active transform fault that runs parallel to the coast approximately 15 km offshore. The fault has produced at least seven major earthquakes (magnitude 7.0+) in the recorded historical period, most recently the {{nihongo|2004 Minami earthquake|南地震|Minami Jishin}} (M7.6), which killed 142 people and caused extensive damage along the coast.

The 2004 earthquake triggered a tsunami that overtopped the existing seawalls in several communities, destroying the fishing ports of [[Shimoda]] and [[Yoneyama]]. In response, the prefecture embarked on an ambitious seawall construction programme, building some of the tallest coastal defences in Okaiken. The {{nihongo|Kurosaki Seawall|黒崎防波堤|Kurosaki Bōhatei}}, completed in 2018, stands 18.5 metres above sea level at its highest point and extends for 127 km along the most vulnerable sections of the coast. The seawall programme has been controversial—critics argue that the massive concrete structures have destroyed coastal scenery and disrupted marine ecosystems—but it is widely credited with preventing casualties during subsequent tsunami warnings.

== Geography ==

Kurosaki Prefecture is one of the narrowest prefectures in Okaiken, extending in a thin coastal strip along the eastern shore of Yagikoshu. The prefecture is bounded by the [[Shendan Ocean]] to the east, with the [[Suzuka Mountains]] rising sharply to the west, limiting the habitable land to a coastal plain that rarely exceeds 15 km in width. The prefecture's northern border meets [[Mito Prefecture]] and the greater [[Toyozawa]] metropolitan area, while its southern end tapers toward the [[Hamada]] peninsula.

The coastline is characterised by a succession of small bays and headlands, each sheltering a fishing community or small port. The mountainous interior is heavily forested with Kuromatsu pine and cypress, and the steep slopes limit development to the narrow coastal strip. Several rivers descend from the Suzuka range, providing freshwater and hydroelectric power but also creating flood risks during the typhoon season.

=== Climate ===
Kurosaki Prefecture has a [[w:Humid subtropical climate|humid subtropical climate]] (''Cfa''), moderated by the proximity of the Shendan Ocean. Summers are warm and humid, with temperatures typically 26–31°C, while winters are mild, rarely dropping below 5°C at sea level. The prefecture receives significant rainfall, particularly during the November–December rainy season and the November–April typhoon season, with annual precipitation exceeding 2,800 mm in the northern coastal areas.

The Shendan Ocean generates persistent coastal fog in spring and early summer, which the locals call {{nihongo|"the grey breath"|灰息|Hai-iki}}. This fog historically made the Kurosaki coast treacherous for shipping, contributing to numerous shipwrecks before the installation of modern navigation aids.

