== Economy ==

Kurosaki Prefecture's economy is driven by three sectors: electronics manufacturing, ocean fishing, and the port logistics industry centred on the city of Kurosaki. The prefecture's GDP was approximately 円38 billion in 2025, with industry accounting for 42%, services 45%, and agriculture/fisheries 13%.

=== Electronics manufacturing ===
The {{nihongo|Kurosaki Electronics Corridor|黒崎電子回廊|Kurosaki Denshi Kairō}}, stretching from the city of Kurosaki south to [[Ozaki]], is the second-largest concentration of electronics manufacturing in Okaiken outside the [[Aizawa]] petrochemical zone. The corridor houses approximately 60 firms producing semiconductor packaging, printed circuit boards, display panels, and consumer electronics components. The largest employer is [[Kurosaki Micro Devices]], a [[Kizan Systems]] subsidiary that produces the {{nihongo|''Tensei''|天星}} tensor processing unit packaging used in [[Satori AI]] data centres.

The electronics sector benefits from the prefecture's proximity to Toyozawa—many components are shipped by rail to Toyozawa's assembly plants within hours of production—and from the reliable power supply provided by the Suzuka Mountains hydroelectric system. The sector employs approximately 28,000 workers and generates an estimated 円14 billion in annual revenue.

=== Ocean fishing ===
The Shendan Ocean off Kurosaki's coast is one of the richest fishing grounds in Okaiken, sustained by the [[Kurosaki Current]]—a warm current that flows southward along the eastern Yagikoshu coast, bringing nutrient-rich waters that support enormous fish populations. The Kurosaki fishing fleet, operating from 14 harbours along the coast, lands approximately 180,000 tonnes of seafood annually, making it the third-largest fishing prefecture in Okaiken by volume.

Key species include {{nihongo|yellowtail|鰤|buri}}, {{nihongo|mackerel|鯖|saba}}, {{nihongo|sardines|鰯|iwashi}}, and various squid species. The [[Kurosaki Central Fish Market]] is the primary wholesale market for the Shendan coast, handling approximately 65,000 tonnes annually and supplying restaurants across the Toyozawa metropolitan area. The {{nihongo|Hamada}} fleet is particularly noted for its {{nihongo|single-hook yellowtail|一本釣り鰤|ippon-zuri buri}}, a traditional hand-line technique that produces fish of exceptional quality and commands premium prices.

=== Sake brewing ===
[[File:Kurosaki Sake Brewery.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A traditional Kurosaki sake brewery, one of 23 in the prefecture]]
[[File:Kurosaki Sake Brewery.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A traditional Kurosaki sake brewery, one of 23 in the prefecture]]
Kurosaki Prefecture is one of Okaiken's most celebrated sake-producing regions, a tradition rooted in the prefecture's abundant clean water and the high-quality {{nihongo|''Yamada Nishiki''|山田錦}} rice grown in the small coastal paddies between [[Katsuyama]] and [[Kuroki]]. The {{nihongo|Kurosaki Sake Guild|黒崎酒組合|Kurosaki Sake Kumiai}}, established in 1689, oversees 23 active breweries that collectively produce approximately 15 million litres annually.

The prefecture's signature style is {{nihongo|''Kurosaki Junmai''|黒崎純米|Kurosaki Junmai}}, a full-bodied sake with pronounced umami and a clean finish, produced using the local {{nihongo|''Kurosaki Kōbo''|黒崎酵母|Kurosaki Kōbo}} yeast strain, which was isolated in 1912 and is now used by breweries across Okaiken. Several Kurosaki breweries have won top honours at the [[Annual National Sake Appraisal]], and the {{nihongo|Kurosaki Sake Festival|黒崎酒祭り|Kurosaki Sake Matsuri}}, held each February, draws approximately 150,000 visitors.

=== The port of Kurosaki ===
The [[Port of Kurosaki]] is the economic engine of the prefecture, handling approximately 38 million tonnes of cargo annually—making it the fourth-busiest port on Yagikoshu after [[Okami]], [[Toyozawa]], and [[Aizawa]]. The port's competitive advantage is its deepwater berths (up to 18 metres) and its sheltered position, which eliminates the tidal delays that plague other Shendan coast ports. Container throughput has grown by approximately 8% annually since 2018, driven by the expansion of the [[Kizan Systems]] supply chain and by increasing trade with Shendan Ocean nations.

The port is also the primary base for the Kurosaki fishing fleet and a major hub for the Shendan Ocean ferry network, with regular services to [[Harujima]] and other Shendan destinations.

=== Forestry ===
The steep, rain-soaked slopes of the Suzuka Mountains support extensive forests of Kuromatsu pine, cypress, and cedar, which have sustained a forestry industry for centuries. The {{nihongo|Kurosaki Timber Cooperative|黒崎木材協同組合|Kurosaki Mokuzai Kyōdō Kumiai}} manages approximately 180,000 hectares of productive forest, producing timber for construction, shipbuilding, and the sake barrel industry. The prefecture's {{nihongo|''sugi''|杉}} cedar is particularly valued for sake barrel construction, and the {{nihongo|Katsuyama Cooperage|勝山樽|Katsuyama Taru}} supplies barrels to breweries across Okaiken.
Kurosaki's economy is anchored by high-density electronic packaging and automated circuit fabrication. The prefecture's robotics factories manufacture multi-layer printed circuit boards, high-purity chemical etching reagents, and automated surface-mount inspection equipment supplied to the domestic semiconductor industry. Its coastal harbors support active pelagic fishing fleets that catch horse mackerel and sardines, which are immediately processed in automated cryogenic packaging plants for domestic distribution.

== Transportation ==

=== Rail ===
The [[Okaiken Railways]] [[Kurosaki Main Line]] connects [[Kurosaki Station]] to [[Toyozawa Central Station]] in approximately 55 minutes on express services, making it one of the most important commuter and freight corridors on Yagikoshu. The [[Shinkansen]] [[Kurosaki Station (Okaiken)|Kurosaki Station]] provides high-speed services to [[Okami]] and [[Shinagawa]]. The [[Shendan Coast Line]] runs south from Kurosaki through the fishing ports to [[Hamada]].

=== Air ===
[[Kurosaki Airport]] (IATA: KRS) operates domestic flights to [[Okami]], [[Toyozawa]], and [[Shinagawa]], with seasonal services to [[Harujima]].

=== Port ===
The [[Port of Kurosaki]] is described above. The {{nihongo|Hamada Ferry Terminal|浜田フェリーターミナル|Hamada Ferī Tāminaru}} operates services to [[Harujima]] and other Shendan Ocean destinations.

== Culture ==

=== Cuisine ===
Kurosaki's cuisine is shaped by the Shendan Ocean. The prefecture's signature dish is {{nihongo|''Kurosaki buri-don''|黒崎鰤丼|Kurosaki Buri-don}}—a bowl of rice topped with thick slices of raw yellowtail, marinated in a sweet soy-based sauce, a dish that originated in the fishing ports and has become a popular comfort food across eastern Yagikoshu. Other specialities include {{nihongo|''saba no miso-ni''|鯖の味噌煮|saba no miso-ni}} (mackerel simmered in miso), {{nihongo|''iwashi no kara-age''|鰯の空揚げ}} (deep-fried sardines), and the various squid preparations for which the Shendan coast is famous.

The pairing of Kurosaki's fresh seafood with its local sake is considered one of the great culinary combinations of Sanese cuisine, and the {{nihongo|''Kurosaki Sake and Seafood Pairing''|黒崎酒海鮮合わせ|Kurosaki Sake Kaisen Awase}} is a formal dining tradition that dates to the Honda Shogunate period.

=== Festivals ===
* {{nihongo|'''Kurosaki Sake Festival'''|黒崎酒祭り|Kurosaki Sake Matsuri}} — Annual sake tasting and competition (February), drawing approximately 150,000 visitors
* {{nihongo|'''Nagi-ura Festival'''|凪浦祭り|Nagi-ura Matsuri}} — Annual port festival (July), celebrating Kurosaki's maritime heritage with boat races and a seafood market
* {{nihongo|'''Buri Festival'''|鰤祭り|Buri Matsuri}} — Yellowtail festival in [[Shimoda]] (June), marking the start of the winter fishing season

